<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Book of Kells</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 800</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kells">Kells</a> is an illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, made around 800, probably in part at the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> and completed at Kells in Ireland. Its elaborate decoration of interlace, animals and human figures makes it the masterpiece of Insular art. It has been kept at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a> since the 17th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-kells.jpg" alt="Book of Kells" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unattributed · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KellsFol032vChristEnthroned.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kells">Kells</a> is one of the latest and finest of the Insular manuscripts made from the late 6th to the early 9th centuries in monasteries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Ireland, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne-gospels">Lindisfarne Gospels</a> and the Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a>. It was created in a Columban monastery in Ireland or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and may have had contributions from several Columban houses. Its fully developed ornament places it in the late 8th or early 9th century. The woad and lichen used for its blue and purple pigments are taken as strong evidence of an origin in modern Scotland.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> record that in 1007 the great Gospel of Columkille was stolen from the sacristy of the stone church at Cenannas on account of its wrought shrine. It was recovered months later under a sod, without its gold and jewelled cover.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> contains the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, with prefaces, summaries and canon tables. It now has 340 leaves of calf vellum, or 680 pages. Its text is largely from the Vulgate, with passages from earlier Vetus Latina versions, and is in Insular majuscule by at least three scribes. Its decoration surpasses that of other Insular Gospel books: ten full-page illustrations and pages full of initials, interlace and human and animal forms in purple, red, green and yellow, with no gold or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> leaf. It seems to have stood on the high altar and been used at Mass, with appearance preferred to practicality, and errors were left uncorrected.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The book stayed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kells">Kells</a> until 1654, when Cromwell's cavalry was quartered in the church and the governor sent it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>. Henry Jones, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Clogher, presented it to Trinity College in 1661. In the 19th century its link with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> was used to argue Ireland's cultural primacy, and its designs influenced the Celtic Revival and were copied in metalwork, embroidery and pottery. It was rebound in four volumes in 1953, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> added it to its Memory of the World International Register in 2011.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Book of Kells” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Book of Kells</b>, a masterpiece of insular manuscript art, was probably made at least in part on Iona around 800.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
